BeeZip: archives from the command line

Usage: beezip [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run           Run any command by name, with JSON arguments
  commands      List every command
  schema        Print a command's input and output JSON schemas
  mcp           Serve BeeZip's tools over MCP on stdin/stdout
  version       Show the BeeZip version and build information.
  add           Add files and folders to an archive (zip, bhive, 7z, tar, cpio and ar, and tar and
                cpio under any codec). Each input is stored under its own name, as `archive.create`
                stores them, in `dir` if given; an ar holds files only. The archive isn't written in
                place: a new one is written beside it and swapped in, so a failed or cancelled add
                leaves it as it was. Entries that stay are copied without decoding. An archive whose
                entries are encrypted needs a password for the new files, unless `unencrypted`.
                [alias: a]
  convert       Write an archive's entries to a new archive in another format, or with other
                options: zip, bhive, 7z, tar, cpio, ar, cab, ISO, squashfs or DMG, tar and cpio
                under a codec too, from any archive BeeZip reads (RAR, packages, disk images and
                split archives included). The source isn't changed. Entries are copied without
                decoding when the formats match and no option asks for something else (`a.tar.gz` →
                `a.tar.zst` keeps every tar entry, encoding the stream again). An encrypted source
                needs `new_password` or `decrypt`: encryption is never dropped silently. What the
                new format can't hold is left out and reported in `skipped`; hard links become files
                where the format has none.
  create        Create an archive from files and folders. Each input is stored under its own name
                (`src/` becomes `src/…`). The format comes from `format` or the output's extension
                (`.zip`, `.bhive`, `.7z`, `.tar`, `.tar.zst`, `.tgz`, `.cpio.gz`, `.ar`, `.cab`,
                `.iso`, `.squashfs`, `.dmg`…); ar and cab hold files only. A `.bhive` is BeeZip's
                own format: it stores repeated content once, reads any file without decoding the
                rest, and uses every core. An ISO has Joliet and Rock Ridge, a DMG is an HFS+ volume
                in a zlib-compressed image. The archive is written to a temporary file and renamed
                into place, so a failed or cancelled create leaves nothing behind. [alias: c]
  extract       Extract an archive, or the selected entries. Unsafe entries (paths climbing out with
                `..`, links leading out) are skipped and the rest extracted; the command then fails
                with `Unsafe` and the report in `details`. Without `dest`, files go into the current
                directory. [alias: x]
  info          Summarise an archive: its format chain, entry counts, sizes and compression ratio,
                methods, encryption, whether it is solid or self-extracting, and its comment.
  list          List an archive's entries. Without `flat`, one folder level at a time (the root, or
                `dir`), with folders that only exist as parts of paths shown too; with `flat` or a
                `glob`, every entry under `dir`. Results come in pages with `offset` and `limit`.
                [alias: l]
  protect       Change who can open an encrypted `.bhive`, without recompressing it: add ways in,
                remove them, or make a new file key, which seals every cell again and rebuilds every
                slot. Removing a way in isn't revoking it: someone who opened the archive before
                could have kept its key; a new key is what shuts them out of later versions.
  protection    List the ways into an encrypted `.bhive` (passwords, recovery keys, keys,
                certificates, This PC, security keys), with their labels, and say whether it resists
                quantum computers. Needs a way in: the archive's descriptions of its ways in are
                encrypted too.
  remove        Remove entries from an archive (zip, bhive, 7z, tar, cpio and ar, and tar and cpio
                under any codec). A folder takes everything under it. A new archive is written
                beside the old one and swapped in, so a failed or cancelled removal leaves it as it
                was; the entries that stay are copied without decoding, except the rest of a 7z
                solid block that loses one, and what is left of a .bhive cell that lost more than
                half its bytes, which are encoded again. Fails with `NotFound` when nothing matches.
                [alias: d]
  rename        Rename an entry of an archive, or a folder and everything under it (zip, bhive, 7z,
                tar, cpio and ar, and tar and cpio under any codec). `to` is the new full path, so a
                rename can also move an entry. The new name mustn't be taken, unless only its case
                differs. Entries are copied without decoding, encrypted ones included; renamed names
                are stored in UTF-8. A new archive is written beside the old one and swapped in.
                [alias: rn]
  sign          Sign a `.bhive`, or remove its signatures (docs/bhive.md §13). A signature says who
                made the archive's contents and that they haven't changed since; changing who can
                open it keeps it, changing the contents drops it. Signing reads every cell once, to
                make its digests or to check the cells against them.
  test          Test an archive's integrity: read every entry to the end, checking CRCs and codec
                checksums. Fails with `Corrupt`, listing the damaged entries in `details`. [alias:
                t]
  verify        Check a `.bhive`'s signatures, and that every cell is what was signed, read to the
                end as `archive.test` reads it. It passes when no signature failed and one is valid:
                from each signer `require_signer` names, or without it from someone you trust
                (yourself, a contact, or a certificate the system trusts). Fails with
                `SignatureRequired` when the archive isn't signed that way or changed after it was
                signed, and `Corrupt` when a cell doesn't match the digest that was signed.
  bench         Benchmark the codecs: compress and decompress a sample in memory with each codec at
                the levels and thread counts of a grid, and report the size, the speed and the
                memory each way. Every round trip is checked against the sample; one that doesn't
                match fails its cell, not the run. Each cell is also sent as a `Partial` job event
                as it finishes, and a cancelled run returns the cells it finished. Nothing is
                written.
  open          Open an archive's file with its default app (Windows' "Open with" dialog when there
                is none). The entry is extracted, with the archive's Mark of the Web, into a folder
                of its own under `%TEMP%/BeeZip/open`, so two entries of one name don't collide;
                folders there older than a day are deleted as it goes. Programs run as they would
                from Explorer: SmartScreen sees the mark.
  read          Read part of one entry without extracting it, as text, hex or base64. An offset is
                reached by decoding up to it, since a compressed entry can't seek.
  detect        Detect what a file is: its chain of codecs and archive format (`zstd → tar`),
                whether it is a self-extracting archive, and what it is when it isn't an archive at
                all.
  formats       List the archive formats and codecs BeeZip reads and writes, with their
                capabilities, methods, levels and threading.
  compress      Compress one file with a single-stream codec (`a.txt` → `a.txt.zst`). The output
                keeps the input's modification time; the input is left in place.
  decompress    Decompress a compressed file, one codec layer per call: `a.tar.zst` → `a.tar`,
                `a.tgz` → `a.tar`, `notes.txt.gz` → `notes.txt`. The output keeps the input's
                modification time; the input is left in place.
  certificates  The certificates archives can be encrypted to and signed with
  keys          Your keys for opening and signing .bhive archives, and your contacts' public keys
  settings      Show and change BeeZip's settings (config.toml)
  shell         BeeZip's Explorer menu: what a click on it does
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -i, --identity <FILE>
          A key file that opens encrypted .bhive archives (age identities, PEM and PKCS #8 keys,
          .pfx files on Windows); repeatable

      --password-stdin
          Read the password or recovery key from the first line of stdin, where other programs can't
          see it as they can the command line

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
